{"task_id": "HumanEval/46", "solution": "```python\ndef fib4(n: int) -> int:\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n    if n == 2:\n        return 2\n    if n == 3:\n        return 0\n\n    f0 = 0\n    f1 = 0\n    f2 = 2\n    f3 = 0\n\n    for _ in range(4, n + 1):\n        next_fib = f3 + f2 + f1 + f0\n        f0 = f1\n        f1 = f2\n        f2 = f3\n        f3 = next_fib\n    \n    return f3\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/47", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n\ndef calculate_median(numbers: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the median of a list of numbers.\n\n    The function handles both odd and even length lists.\n    - For odd length lists, it returns the middle element after sorting.\n    - For even length lists, it returns the average of the two middle elements after sorting.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of numerical values (int or float).\n\n    Returns:\n        The median of the list as a float.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty to calculate the median.\")\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_numbers)\n\n    if n % 2 == 1:\n        middle_index = n // 2\n        return float(sorted_numbers[middle_index])\n    else:\n        mid1_index = n // 2 - 1\n        mid2_index = n // 2\n        return (sorted_numbers[mid1_index] + sorted_numbers[mid2_index]) / 2.0\n```"}
